ome; limitation: requires separation at source and markets for recyclables), and controlled incineration (advantage: greatly reduces volume and destroys pathogens; limitation: smoke and air pollution, high cost). Each method is described clearly, and one advantage and one limitation are explicitly tied to community conditions such as cost, land or labour.' AND e.marking_rubric IS NULL AND e.required_points IS '["Four distinct waste management methods correctly described","One advantage stated for each method","One limitation stated for each method"]' AND e.optional_points IS '["Reference to community-level cost or labour constraints","A concluding recommendation of the most suitable mix of methods"]' AND e.ai_grading_enabled IS '1')) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;
INSERT INTO _migration_365_guard(valid) SELECT CASE WHEN NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM questions q WHERE q.id = 'q_sci_2024_2_008' AND NOT (q.topic_id IS 'topic_wassce_p2_sci_env' AND q.subject_id IS 'subj_wassce_int_science' AND q.exam_type_id IS 'exam_wassce' AND q.paper_type_id IS 'paper_wassce_2' AND q.past_paper_id IS 'pp_wassce_sci_2024_2' AND q.question_text IS 'Discuss the causes and effects of water pollution in Ghanaian communities and suggest two measures that can be taken to control it.' AND q.question_type IS 'essay' AND q.round_type IS NULL AND q.options IS NULL AND q.correct_answer IS 'A strong answer names causes such as dumping of refuse and sewage into streams, chemical fertiliser and pesticide run-off from farms, illegal mining (galamsey) washing mercury and silt into rivers, and oil or soapy water from washing at riverbanks. Effects include water-borne diseases such as cholera and typhoid, death of fish and other aquatic life as oxygen levels fall, siltation that raises the cost of treating pipe-borne water, and loss of fishing livelihoods. Control measures: enforcing by-laws and pollution penalties with regular monitoring of water bodies, and educating communities while providing proper refuse collection so waste is not dumped into rivers; buffer-zone tree planting along riverbanks is also creditable.' AND q.explanation IS 'A strong answer names causes such as dumping of refuse and sewage into streams, chemical fertiliser and pesticide run-off from farms, illegal mining (galamsey) washing mercury and silt into rivers, and oil or soapy water from washing at riverbanks. Effects include water-borne diseases such as cholera and typhoid, death of fish and other aquatic life as oxygen levels fall, siltation that raises the cost of treating pipe-borne water, and loss of fishing livelihoods. Control measures: enforcing by-laws and pollution penalties with regular monitoring of water bodies, and educating communities while providing proper refuse collection 